The C library's own signals stay out of programs' masks (after patch
0022). sigprocmask and pthread_sigmask gave the kernel a program's set
as it was, so a set filled by hand (memset to all ones, as some programs
make "every signal") blocked SIGCANCEL and SIGTIMER, and a SIG_SETMASK
with a mask read back earlier, which never holds them, unblocked
SIGCANCEL in a thread that had disabled cancellation, so a cancel that
came then ran its handler and cancelled the thread anyway; mlibc's own
ppoll, wordexp and siglongjmp do that SIG_SETMASK. Now a program's set
neither blocks nor unblocks the signals from 32 up to SIGRTMIN, as in
glibc and musl: SIG_SETMASK keeps them as the thread has them (one more
call to read the mask), SIG_BLOCK and SIG_UNBLOCK leave them out
(mlibc::program_sigset), and pthread_setcancelstate asks the kernel
itself. Meant for upstream.

--- a/options/internal/include/mlibc/sigset.hpp
+++ b/options/internal/include/mlibc/sigset.hpp
@@ -8,4 +8,13 @@
 // from 32 up to SIGRTMIN (SIGCANCEL, SIGTIMER), which programs cannot add.
 int sigaddset_internal(sigset_t *sigset, int sig);
 
+// The set a program's sigprocmask() or pthread_sigmask() gives the
+// kernel, in `copy`: `set` with the C library's own signals as they are
+// in `now`, the calling thread's mask, so that a program can neither
+// block nor unblock them. `now` is needed for SIG_SETMASK alone, and
+// may be null otherwise. Returns `set` itself for a `how` that is no
+// operation, for the kernel to refuse.
+const sigset_t *program_sigset(int how, const sigset_t *set, const sigset_t *now,
+		sigset_t *copy);
+
 } // namespace mlibc
--- a/options/internal/generic/sigset.cpp
+++ b/options/internal/generic/sigset.cpp
@@ -74,6 +74,20 @@
 	return 0;
 }
 
+const sigset_t *mlibc::program_sigset(int how, const sigset_t *set, const sigset_t *now,
+		sigset_t *copy) {
+	if(how != SIG_BLOCK && how != SIG_UNBLOCK && how != SIG_SETMASK)
+		return set;
+	*copy = *set;
+	for(int sig = 32; sig < SIGRTMIN; sig++) {
+		if(how == SIG_SETMASK && sigismember(now, sig))
+			sigaddset_internal(copy, sig);
+		else
+			sigdelset(copy, sig);
+	}
+	return copy;
+}
+
 int sigaddset(sigset_t *sigset, int sig) {
 	if(sig >= 32 && sig < SIGRTMIN) {
 		errno = EINVAL;
